    card title "A Martian's map"

Why do firms exist at all, if markets work so well (@draw simon@)? Herbert Simon's visitor from Mars, the one who  (@draw greens@)saw firms as green areas and mark (@draw market ends@)ets as red lines, points the way.
    draw sea land mainland parallel true

B: Point its telescope at Harbourtown. Th (@draw yard_lab@)e boatyard is a big green patc (@draw bakery_lab bank_lab@)h, Mia's bakery a small one, and red l (@flow r1 r2 r3 r4 r5@)ines run between them a (@draw main_lab market_lab@)nd out to the mainland.

    erase simon

Inside the green there are only faint blue lines: who reports to whom. No prices in there at all.
    draw staff orders_lab

B: Simon bet its report home would speak of large green areas (@highlight greens@) interconnected by red lines, not a web of r (@highlight market@)ed lines with a few green dots. Is it true?
    draw quote

    erase sea land mainland main_lab greens yard_lab bank_lab bakery_lab market market_lab staff orders_lab quote

One check: count the workers who are employees, working inside the green for someone else, rather than for themselves.
    draw axes src

B: Across the who (@draw bar_2@)le world it's just over half. In the poorest countries, about one worker in  (@draw bar_1 ends@)five.

Now guess Norway.

    erase src

    ask "Drag Norway's bar to your guess: what share of its workers are employees?" on bar_5 store g default "50" pool false
        right: Yes: ninety-five percent.
        wrong: You said {g} percent: it's ninety-five.

Ninety-five percent. The United States, nin (@draw bar_4@)ety-four; Germ (@draw src@)any, ninety-two (@draw bar_3@). That counts people working for th (@draw statecue@)e state too, but most work in firms.

B: So in a rich economy almost everyone is directed by a boss, not by a price. Why, if prices coordinate so well?
    highlight bar_3 bar_4 bar_5

    pause 0.5

## Coase's question
sources:
    - id: coase1937
      title: The Nature of the Firm
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1937
      finding: '''The main reason why it is profitable to establish a firm would seem to be that there is a cost of using the price mechanism'' — discovering prices, negotiating and concluding a separate contract for each exchange; a workman moves from department Y to X ''because he is ordered to''; quotes D. H. Robertson''s ''islands of conscious power in this ocean of unconscious co-operation'' (Economica 4(16): 386–405).'
    - id: robertson1923
      title: The Control of Industry
      authors: D. H. Robertson
      year: 1923
      finding: Firms as 'islands of conscious power in this ocean of unconscious co-operation like lumps of butter coagulating in a pail of buttermilk' (p. 85, quoted by Coase 1937).
    - id: econlib_coase
      title: Ronald H. Coase (Concise Encyclopedia of Economics)
      authors: Econlib
      year: 2008
      finding: Coase conceived the 1937 article as an undergraduate on a trip to the United States; he visited Ford and General Motors and asked how economists could say Lenin was wrong that the Russian economy could be run like one big factory.
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/Enc/bios/Coase.html

