    card title "Coase's question"

In the early 1930s a young British student, Ronald Coase, crossed to  (@draw trip@)America to look for an answer.
    draw coase

B: Yes, the same Coase who wrote abo (@draw notct@)ut pollution rights and lighthouses. This is a different idea, and his first.

    erase coase notct trip

Lenin ha (@draw lenin@)d said Russia could be run as one big factory, and (@draw imposs@) Western economists called that impossible (@draw factory fordgm@). Yet Ford and General Motors were run from the top, and ran well.

B: And inside them, (@draw deptY deptX mover@) Coase wrote, a worker who moves from department Y to department X doesn (@move mover by [480,0] duration 1.5@)'t go because relative prices changed. He goes b (@draw ordered@)ecause he is ordered to.

    erase lenin factory fordgm deptY deptX mover ordered imposs

His answer, published in 19 (@draw cue1@)37: there is a cost of using the price mechanism.

    move cue1 by [0,195] scale 0.75 duration 1

B: Take the boat (@draw boat tasks@)yard, building one boat. It could buy every task on the mark (@draw sellers parallel true@)et: the planks, the engine fitting, the paint, the rigging.

Each deal (@draw tags@) means finding the  (@flow m1 m2 m3 m4@)right price, haggling, writing a contract, and checking the work.

    erase tags

B: Say each deal costs fifty dollars of time and lawyers, illustrative numbers. Four deals, two hundred dollars,  (@draw sc_line_2@)and the same again for the next boat.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

    erase sc

Or the yard hires the four workers once,  (@draw island orders@)on one contract each, and tells them every morning what to do.
    fade sellers to 0.15

    erase cue1

B: Many deals replaced by a few long contracts. Those deal costs are the transac (@draw cue2@)tion costs that also block bargains over pollution.

Coase borrowed an image from Dennis Ro (@draw rob@)bertson: firms are islands of conscious power in this ocean of unconscious co-operation.

B: So an island forms wherever telling someone is cheaper than buying from them.
    highlight island

    pause 0.5

## Where the island ends
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - y = c*x
      - y = m
    params:
      m: {value: 60, min: 20, max: 100, step: 5, label: market m, color: '#b5482e'}
      c: {value: 10, min: 4, max: 20, step: 1, label: organising c, color: '#2f6b8f'}
    controls: panel
    show_equation: false
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 12
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 120
    x_label: ' '
    y_label: $ per task
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: m/c, curve: 0, id: edge, label: boundary}
sources:
    - id: coase1937
      title: The Nature of the Firm
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1937
      finding: 'Asks ''why is not all production carried on by one big firm?''; a firm expands until the cost of organising an extra transaction within it equals the cost of carrying it out by exchange on the open market; ''diminishing returns to management''; the telephone and telegraph, by reducing the cost of organising spatially, tend to increase the size of the firm (Economica 4(16): 386–405).'

    text lab_in "organise inside" #2f6b8f at.data [9,113] font_size 28 hidden true
    text lab_mk "use the market" #b5482e at.data [1.7,66] font_size 28 hidden true
    text drm "diminishing returns\nto management" #8f887c at.data [3.4,108] cites ["coase1937"] font_size 28 hidden true
    icon phone at.data [2,100] size 70 set twemoji icon_look picture of telephone hidden true
    text readout "firm's edge: {eq.edge_x:0} tasks" at.data [4.8,84] font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch mth at.data [3.6,88] work [{"tex":"\\text{inside: } c\\,x \\qquad \\text{market: } m"},{"tex":"\\text{inside while } c\\,x < m"},{"tex":"x^{*} = m / c"},"60 ÷ 10 = 6  ·  60 ÷ 6 = 10"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text q37 "“why is not all production carried on\nby one big firm?” Coase, 1937" #8f887c at.data [7,95] cites ["coase1937"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text xcap "tasks inside →" #3d3833 at.data [11.7,9] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Where the island ends"

    hide x_label

If giving orders saves so much, why not one giant firm? Coase ask (@draw q37@)ed it too: why is not all production carried on by one big firm?
    draw axes xcap

    erase q37

B: Because orders get dearer (@draw curve_0@) as the firm grows. Each extra task is one more thi (@draw lab_in@)ng for the boss to oversee, and mistakes creep in.

Economists call it di (@draw drm@)minishing returns to management. Against it stands the cost of buying a task on the marke (@draw curve_1 lab_mk ends@)t: flat, say sixty dollars.

    erase drm

B: Taking a task inside pays as lo (@point at.data [3,30] gesture tap@)ng as organising it costs less than buying it.

So the yard grows until the two  (@draw edge readout@)costs meet, here at six tasks. That is Coase's rule for where the firm ends.

B: Coase also named something that moves it: (@draw phone@) the telephone, which makes organising at a distance cheaper.

    quiz "The telephone makes organising at a distance cheaper. What happens to the boatyard?" voice b pool false
        right: It grows: the organising line falls, so the crossing moves right.
        wrong: Which line does the telephone move, and which way does the crossing go? Here, it grows.
        + It grows
        * It shrinks
        * No change

It grows. The (@animate params.c 6 duration 2.5 ghost true@) organising line tips down, and the meeting point moves out to ten tasks.

    erase phone

    draw panel

B: Your turn. Drag the market cost down, as if finding a seller suddenly got cheap, and watch the boatyard shrink.
    explore

Cheaper markets, smaller islands; cheaper organising, bigger ones. The firm's edge is a cost comparison, not a law of nature.
    animate params.m 60 params.c 6 duration 1

    erase readout

B: Want the boundary in one line of algebra?
    fade axes curves edge lab_in lab_mk panel xcap to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store mq default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

Organising the x-th task inside costs c times x. Buying it costs m.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

B: So the yard takes a task inside while c times x is below m.
    draw mth_line_2

The edge is where they are equal: x star equals m over c.
    draw mth_line_3

B: Sixty over ten is six tasks. With the telephone, sixty over six is ten.
    draw mth_line_4

    pause 0.5

    erase mth

@after

    erase mathq

    fade axes curves edge lab_in lab_mk panel xcap to 1

    draw readout

    pause 0.5

## Is a boss just a buyer?
sources:
    - id: alchian1972
      title: Production, Information Costs, and Economic Organization
      authors: Armen A. Alchian and Harold Demsetz
      year: 1972
      finding: 'The firm ''has no power of fiat, no authority, no disciplinary action any different in the slightest degree from ordinary market contracting''; an employer can fire or sue, as a customer can ''fire'' a grocer by buying elsewhere; the firm is a team with a central monitor who is residual claimant (American Economic Review 62(5): 777–795).'
    - id: simon1991
      title: Organizations and Markets
      authors: Herbert A. Simon
      year: 1991
      finding: 'New institutional economics leaves out authority, identification with the organisation and coordination; employees accept authority over a range of tasks (Journal of Economic Perspectives 5(2): 25–44).'
    - id: coase1972
      title: 'Industrial Organization: A Proposal for Research'
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1972
      finding: Coase notes that 'The Nature of the Firm' was 'much cited and little used' (in V. Fuchs, ed., Policy Issues and Research Opportunities in Industrial Organization, NBER).
    - id: nobel1991
      title: The Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel 1991
      authors: Nobel Prize Outreach
      year: 1991
      finding: Awarded to Ronald H. Coase for his discovery and clarification of the significance of transaction costs and property rights for the institutional structure and functioning of the economy.
      url: https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/1991/summary/

    text cghost "Coase: orders, not prices" #8f887c x 500 y 625 font_size 38 style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
