    card title "Is a boss just a buyer?"

So the firm's edge sits where two costs meet. But is an order really so different from a purchase?
    draw cghost

B: Armen Alc (@draw ad_head ad1@)hian and Harold Demsetz said no, in 1972. A firm, they wrote, has (@highlight ad1@) no power of fiat.

B: The boss can fire you or sue you. You can fire (@draw ad2@) your grocer just as easily, by shopping somewhere else.

To them a firm is a bundle of contracts  (@draw ad3@)with a boss in the middle, there to watch a team whose sepa (@highlight ad_head effect box@)rate efforts are hard to measure.

B: Simon disagre (@draw si_head si1@)ed. Employees accept authority over tasks no contract could list in advance, and many come  (@draw si2@)to identify with the firm.

And neither side says which costs decide where a firm stops. Why does a carmaker build some parts and buy others? Tha (@draw next@)t's make or buy, a question of its own.
    highlight ad3 si1

B: How sure are we? That firms exis (@draw st1@)t because using markets has costs: mostly agreed.

Which costs matter most, (@draw st2@) and whether an order differs from a purchase: contested.

B: Coase grumbled in 1972 that his pa (@draw cited@)per was much cited and little used; in 1991 it helped win him the Nobel prize.
    erase cghost

    erase cghost ad_head ad1 ad2 ad3 si_head si1 si2 next st1 st2 cited

    draw sorter

    ask "Order or price? Sort each move into where it happens." on sorter store f stage own voice b
        right: All four. Inside a firm someone is told; between firms, someone is paid a price.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try: told, welder and clerks; paid, flour and sails.

    erase sorter

    draw bx parallel true

    quiz "A newspaper stops employing photographers and buys each photo from freelancers through an app. What most likely fell?"
        right: The cost of using the market: the app made each deal cheap, so the paper buys instead of employing.
        wrong: Coase's question is what it costs to use the market instead of an order. Here: the cost of finding and paying each seller.
        + The cost of finding and paying a seller for each photo
        * The price of cameras
        * Photographers' wages

    quiz "A software firm doubles in size and its managers drown in meetings. By Coase's logic, what should it do?" voice b
        right: Buy more from outside: organising has grown dearer than the market, so the edge moves in.
        wrong: Which cost rose, organising or buying? Here: buy more tasks from outside.
        + Buy more tasks from outside
        * Take even more tasks inside
        * Nothing: size does not matter

    fade bx_org bx_e1 bx_e1l to 0.25

All those meetings st (@draw bx_steep bx_e2 bx_e2l parallel true@)eepen the organising line, so the edge moves in.

    erase bx bx_steep bx_e2 bx_e2l

B: So why do firms exist if markets work so well? Be (@draw fl ml@)cause using a market isn't free.
    draw isl lines parallel true

Where tell (@highlight isl@)ing is cheaper than buying, the green islands grow. Wher (@highlight lines@)e it isn't, the red lines take over.

    pause 0.5

